<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/expert/computational-materials-design">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is the summit of the Materials Science track, and it stands on three earlier
    lessons. From the intermediate tier, <em>Atoms to crystals</em> taught the bonding models —
    Lewis structures, shapes, orbitals — that every computational method here either makes
    rigorous or descends from. From the advanced tier, <em>Semiconductors and doping</em>
    gave you the band gap as a physical quantity, here the single most computed number in
    the field, and <em>Phase diagrams</em> supplied the stability logic that explains why
    predicted compounds still fail in the furnace. What is new is the pipeline: discovery
    run as engineering.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why Lewis structures and VSEPR count as the first computational models of matter, and where the heuristic stops;</li>
    <li>read a band structure argument — how molecular orbital theory scales to solids and what the band gap decides;</li>
    <li>describe DFT's shortcut (Hohenberg–Kohn, Kohn–Sham) along with its known blind spots;</li>
    <li>assess database-plus-ML screening, including why ML surrogates interpolate well and extrapolate poorly;</li>
    <li>explain inverse design and the self-driving laboratory, and why the furnace keeps the final word.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational materials design is the field's culmination: <strong>discovery as a pipeline
    — predict with quantum mechanics, accelerate with machine learning, verify with autonomous
    experiments</strong>. The serendipity era (penicillin, Teflon, most alloys) is giving way to
    engineered search. But the pipeline's inputs are the bonding models this subject has taught
    for a century — Lewis structures, VSEPR shapes, molecular orbitals, hybridisation,
    intermolecular forces — because every computational method is either one of those models
    made rigorous or their quantum-mechanical descendant. The chapter therefore runs both
    directions at once: the classical models as intuition, and the machinery that turns them
    into numbers good enough to bet research budgets on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the genome idea, DFT, ML acceleration, the autonomous loop" widget="equationStep" config={compmatCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels are the pipeline itself, in running order.
    Panel 1 states the strategy — compute instead of synthesising every candidate — and panels
    2 and 3 are its two speed multipliers: DFT making quantum prediction affordable, then
    machine learning making DFT predictions instant. Watch the units in the notes: CPU-days
    collapsing to milliseconds is the whole acceleration in one phrase. Panel 4 closes the
    loop with robots, and its honest framing matters — the cycle ends in a furnace, not a
    printout.
  </p>

  <h2>Lewis structures and VSEPR: the first computational models of matter</h2>
  <p>
    The Lewis structure (1916) is the original back-of-envelope simulation: count valence
    electrons, place bonds and lone pairs, satisfy octets, mark resonance where multiple
    placements are valid (ozone's two equal bonds, benzene's delocalised ring). It is crude —
    a bookkeeping device — and it remains the first thing any practising chemist computes,
    because it predicts connectivity and charge distribution in seconds. <strong>VSEPR</strong>
    (valence-shell electron-pair repulsion) adds three-dimensional form: electron pairs
    repel, so they spread maximally — four pairs give the tetrahedron (109.5°, methane), three
    the trigonal plane (120°, formaldehyde), six the octahedron (90°, SF₆) — and lone pairs,
    held by one nucleus instead of two, spread wider and compress bond angles (water: two
    bonds plus two lone pairs, 104.5° instead of 109.5°). The method's power is the
    translation it licenses: geometry from a two-dimensional count. Shape then controls
    function — polarity (CO₂ is linear and nonpolar; H₂O bent and polar, which is why one
    extinguishes flames and the other dissolves salts), and every binding affinity a drug
    designer computes. The expert's caveat is honest: VSEPR is a <em>heuristic</em> that
    fails on transition-metal complexes and hypervalent species — useful exactly as far as
    its model holds, which is the lesson every later method inherits.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the classical models are genuinely computational — Lewis bookkeeping predicts connectivity, VSEPR turns a two-dimensional count into three-dimensional geometry, and shape controls polarity and binding. The next section goes one level down, to the quantum refinements that make those shapes and properties derivable rather than heuristic.</p>

  <h2>Hybridisation and molecular orbitals: the quantum picture underneath</h2>
  <p>
    Two refinements turn the sketches into quantum mechanics. <strong>Hybridisation</strong>
    explains geometry from the atom's side: mixing one s and three p orbitals gives four
    equivalent sp³ lobes pointing tetrahedrally (carbon in diamond and methane); sp² mixing
    gives three planar lobes plus a perpendicular p orbital (graphene, benzene, and the
    conjugated systems whose delocalised electrons make semiconductors and dyes work); sp
    gives the linear triple bond (acetylene). The hybridisation a carbon adopts is thus a
    one-letter code for its local dimensionality — read it off and you know the geometry.
    <strong>Molecular orbital theory</strong> goes further: atomic orbitals combine into
    bonding orbitals (lower energy, electron density between nuclei) and antibonding orbitals
    (higher, with a node between); fill them from the bottom and the molecule's stability,
    magnetism (O₂'s two unpaired electrons — the Lewis structure cannot explain paramagnetism;
    MO theory predicts it) and reactivity all fall out. For materials, the same formalism
    scales to solids: ~10<sup>23</sup> orbitals smear into <strong>bands</strong>, the
    filled valence band and empty conduction band, and the <em>band gap</em> between them is
    the number that decides insulator, semiconductor or metal — the single most computed
    number in materials design.
  </p>

  <h2>Intermolecular forces: the soft interactions simulations must capture</h2>
  <p>
    Between molecules, weaker forces organise matter: <strong>dispersion (London)
    forces</strong> — instantaneous dipoles from fluctuating electron clouds, present
    everywhere, growing with polarisability (iodine solid, Teflon's slippery surface);
    <strong>dipole–dipole</strong> attractions between polar molecules; and the
    <strong>hydrogen bond</strong> (H bonded to N, O or F attracted to a lone pair), worth
    roughly 10–40 kJ/mol against covalent bonds' 150–1,000. The magnitudes are the design
    information: water's hydrogen bonding buys a boiling point 180 degrees higher than its
    molecular weight predicts; DNA's double helix is held by base-pair hydrogen bonds
    (individually weak, jointly strong, and breakable one at a time — which is how
    replication works); a gecko climbs on dispersion forces multiplied over billions of
    nanoscale contacts. In simulation these forces are the difference between success and
    failure: molecular dynamics packages model them with empirical potentials, drug-design
    docking scores live or die on them, and the hardest error in computational design is
    systematically missing an interaction the potential never contained — the simulated
    molecule that "binds" only because the model could not see the water it displaces.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "weak forces are negligible compared with covalent bonds."
    Negligible per pair, decisive per mole: proteins fold, water boils, geckos climb, and
    diamond-hard nacre draws its toughness from interfaces — all governed by the "weak"
    interactions, because material behaviour happens exactly where molecules meet. Materials
    design fails silently whenever it optimises the strong bonds and forgets the interfaces.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the model ladder runs from Lewis sketches through hybridisation and molecular orbitals up to bands and the band gap — and the soft intermolecular forces turn out to carry the design-relevant behaviour at every interface. What has been missing is quantitative power: quantum mechanics applied to a whole solid. That is DFT's bargain.</p>

  <h2>DFT: quantum mechanics as an engineering tool</h2>
  <p>
    The exact quantum mechanics of a solid is hopeless: the Schrödinger equation for a
    mole of interacting electrons cannot be solved exactly for even a handful of atoms
    (10 interacting electrons already exceeds any conceivable memory if solved brute-force).
    The workaround that earned Walter Kohn the 1998 Nobel Prize in Chemistry (with John
    Pople) is <strong>density functional theory (DFT)</strong>: the Hohenberg–Kohn theorems
    (1964) prove the ground-state electron <em>density</em> determines everything about the
    system, and the Kohn–Sham scheme (1965) replaces the intractable interacting-electron
    problem with a tractable set of non-interacting electrons moving in an approximate
    potential. The result is quantum-mechanical prediction at engineering cost: from the
    periodic table alone, DFT computes lattice constants within a few percent, formation
    energies, elastic moduli, and band structures — one calculation running overnight to
    days on a cluster, not years of synthesis. Its known blind spots are part of the craft:
    band gaps are systematically underestimated (the "band-gap problem", patched by hybrid
    functionals and GW methods), dispersion needs corrections, and strongly correlated
    systems (transition-metal oxides) misbehave without patches like DFT+U. The practitioner
    knows the error bars the way a machinist knows the machine's backlash.
  </p>

  <h2>Databases and machine learning: screening at industrial scale</h2>
  <p>
    DFT is fast enough to screen and slow enough to bottleneck — one structure costs CPU-hours,
    and interesting design spaces hold millions of candidates. The industrial answer has two
    layers. First, <strong>open databases</strong>: the Materials Project (2011 onward, US
    Materials Genome Initiative) publishes computed properties for over 150,000 inorganic
    compounds; NOMAD, OQMD and others push past a million — the field's data commons, with
    every calculation reproducible from its inputs. Second, <strong>machine-learning
    surrogates</strong>: graph neural networks trained on DFT outputs predict properties in
    milliseconds instead of CPU-days — 5–6 orders of magnitude faster — letting searches
    sweep spaces DFT could never enumerate. DeepMind's GNoME (2023) demonstrated the scale:
    deep learning guided by active learning proposed 2.2 million new crystal structures, of
    which roughly 380,000 sit on the computed stability frontier — multiplying the known
    stable inorganic crystals (about 48,000 from a century of experiment) by several times in
    one publication. The caveat that keeps the field honest: ML models interpolate well and
    extrapolate poorly (the AI evaluation lesson's distribution shift, in a flask), so
    training-set coverage bounds trust, and the strongest predictions remain hypotheses until
    a furnace agrees.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "computers will replace experiments." They replace
    <em>search</em>, not verification: predicted compounds routinely fail to synthesise
    (metastability, competing phases, kinetic barriers DFT's 0-kelvin ground state never
    sees). The Berkeley A-Lab's 2023 run is the honest scoreboard: of 58 crystals its
    algorithm proposed and tried to make autonomously over 17 days, 41 formed — an
    extraordinary hit rate for new materials, and a 30% miss rate no serious design loop
    pretends away. Computation narrows the funnel; the furnace still gets the final word.
  </p>

  <h2>Inverse design and the autonomous laboratory</h2>
  <p>
    Forward screening (propose → compute → filter) is being superseded by <strong>inverse
    design</strong>: specify the property and search structure space for it — generative
    models proposing crystals with target band gaps, optimisation loops steering composition
    toward a modulus, Bayesian optimisation choosing the next experiment to maximise
    information. The loop closes with <strong>self-driving laboratories</strong>: robot
    synthesis plus automated characterisation (X-ray diffraction, calorimetry) plus an
    learning algorithm choosing the next sample — the design–make–test cycle, which once
    took a PhD student a year, compressed to days. High-entropy alloys (five or more metals
    in near-equal mix — a combinatorial space of millions no experimental programme could
    enumerate) and solid-state battery electrolytes are the flagship outputs: both were
    computationally invisible before, both now have industrial programmes. The MGI's 2011
    founding premise — halve the time from discovery to deployment by making computation a
    first-class citizen — has largely been met, and the remaining bottleneck moved where
    bottlenecks go: to data quality, synthesis robotics, and the judgement of which
    properties are worth asking for.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "machine learning has made experiments optional." The
  models interpolate within their training manifolds and inherit their biases; every
  high-profile ML-suggested material (from screened photonics candidates to battery
  electrolytes) still needed synthesis to exist and measurement to be believed — several
  prominent database entries failed independent reproduction. The accurate division of
  labour: computation narrows a candidate space by orders of magnitude, experiments
  decide what is real.
  </p>
  <p><strong>So far:</strong> DFT made quantum prediction an engineering tool, databases and ML surrogates lifted screening to billions of candidates, and inverse design with self-driving laboratories compressed the design–make–test cycle from a PhD-length year to days — with every stage still ending in synthesis and measurement. Before the frontier arguments, check the pipeline's parts.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In one sentence each: what does VSEPR predict, and what is its known failure mode?</li>
    <li>A DFT screening run proposes a crystal whose computed band gap matches the target exactly, but synthesis fails in the furnace. Give two reasons from the lesson why computation can over-predict success.</li>
    <li>Why does molecular orbital theory succeed where the Lewis structure fails for O₂ — and why does that success matter for materials design specifically?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) VSEPR predicts geometry from repulsion — electron pairs
    spread maximally, so four pairs give the tetrahedron at 109.5 degrees, and lone pairs
    compress the angles (water at 104.5 degrees). It is a heuristic that fails on
    transition-metal complexes and hypervalent species — useful exactly as far as its model
    holds. (2) Predicted compounds routinely fail to synthesise because of metastability,
    competing phases and kinetic barriers that DFT's 0-kelvin ground state never sees; and if
    an ML surrogate proposed the candidate, remember it interpolates within its training
    coverage and extrapolates poorly. The A-Lab's 41 of 58 is the honest shape of the funnel —
    a 30% miss rate is normal. (3) The Lewis structure cannot explain why O₂ is paramagnetic;
    filling bonding and antibonding orbitals from the bottom predicts the two unpaired
    electrons. The same formalism scales to solids as bands, and the band gap it yields is
    the number that decides insulator, semiconductor or metal — the most computed number in
    the field.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Discovery is becoming a pipeline: predict with quantum mechanics, accelerate with machine learning, verify with autonomous experiments.</li>
    <li>Lewis structures and VSEPR are the first simulations of matter — every later method is one of these models made rigorous or their quantum descendant.</li>
    <li>Molecular orbital theory scales to solids as bands; the band gap decides insulator, semiconductor or metal.</li>
    <li>DFT (Hohenberg–Kohn, Kohn–Sham) trades the impossible many-electron problem for a tractable one, with known blind spots: band gaps, dispersion, strongly correlated systems.</li>
    <li>Open databases plus ML surrogates moved screening from thousands to billions of candidates — GNoME alone proposed 2.2 million structures.</li>
    <li>Weak forces are negligible per pair and decisive per mole: design fails silently when it optimises strong bonds and forgets interfaces.</li>
    <li>Computation narrows the funnel; the furnace gets the final word — 41 of 58 A-Lab proposals formed in 17 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The subject's other expert lesson, <strong>Nanomaterials and biomaterials</strong>, takes
    the same design logic to the regimes where size and softness dominate — the very interfaces
    and weak interactions this lesson warned that simulations miss most easily. The two lessons
    bracket the field: one designs from the quantum ground state up, the other from function
    and environment down.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Accuracy frontiers in electronic structure.</strong> DFT's functionals are an
    unresolved research programme (the exact functional is proven to exist and remains
    unknown — "the exact DFT problem"); excited states need beyond-DFT methods (time-dependent
    DFT, GW/BSE for optical properties), and strongly correlated matter (high-Tc
    superconductors, Mott insulators) resists the whole framework — the reason quantum
    Monte Carlo and, prospectively, quantum computers (simulating quantum systems is the
    field's canonical quantum-computing application) stay on the roadmap. The materials
    genome is a genome with sequenced exons and uncharted introns.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The data problem underneath the AI.</strong> ML surrogates inherit every defect of
    their training data: DFT's systematic errors become the model's priors, negative results
    (compounds that failed to synthesise) are barely archived anywhere, and cross-database
    comparisons surface inconsistencies measured in tenths of electron-volts — material to
    the design decisions being made. The frontier responses are standard datasets and
    benchmarks, uncertainty-quantified models that report their own confidence, and
    foundation models for materials (pretrained across composition, structure and property
    modalities) — each an application of the machine-learning evaluation discipline to a
    domain where the ground truth costs a furnace run.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The philosophical endgame.</strong> The field's trajectory — models from Lewis to
    DFT to learned surrogates — is a case study in the epistemology of simulation: each
    generation encodes more physics and less intuition, and the expert's role shifts from
    computing properties to interrogating them (is this prediction within the model's
    domain? does the surrogate agree with the physics where both exist? what experiment
    would falsify this fastest?). Structure determines properties became properties determine
    structure — inverse design — and the discipline that began by drawing dots around atoms
    now specifies substances that do not yet exist, then builds machines to argue with the
    universe about them.
  </p>

